## Solution

Let $S = 1! + 2! + \cdots + 2016!$. We seek $\left\lfloor \dfrac{2017!}{S}\right\rfloor$.

**Rewrite.** Since $2017! = 2017 \cdot 2016!$,

$$\frac{2017!}{S} = \frac{2017}{S/2016!}.$$

Set $t = S/2016!$. Then

$$t = \sum_{k=1}^{2016}\frac{k!}{2016!} = \sum_{k=1}^{2016}\frac{1}{(k+1)(k+2)\cdots 2016} = 1 + \frac{1}{2016} + \frac{1}{2015\cdot 2016} + \frac{1}{2014\cdot 2015\cdot 2016} + \cdots$$

So $\dfrac{2017!}{S} = \dfrac{2017}{t}$, and we need to pin down where $\dfrac{2017}{t}$ lies.

**Upper bound: $\dfrac{2017}{t} < 2016$.**

Since every term beyond the first two is positive,

$$t > 1 + \frac{1}{2016} = \frac{2017}{2016}.$$

Therefore

$$\frac{2017}{t} < \frac{2017}{2017/2016} = 2016.$$

**Lower bound: $\dfrac{2017}{t} > 2015$.**

We need $t < \dfrac{2017}{2015} = 1 + \dfrac{2}{2015}$. Bound the tail of $t$:

$$t = 1 + \frac{1}{2016} + \underbrace{\frac{1}{2015\cdot 2016} + \frac{1}{2014\cdot 2015\cdot 2016} + \cdots}_{R}.$$

Every additional factor in the products of $R$ is at most $2015$, so

$$R \le \frac{1}{2015\cdot 2016}\left(1 + \frac{1}{2015} + \frac{1}{2015^2} + \cdots\right) = \frac{1}{2015\cdot 2016}\cdot\frac{2015}{2014} = \frac{1}{2016\cdot 2014}.$$

Hence

$$t \le 1 + \frac{1}{2016} + \frac{1}{2016\cdot 2014} = 1 + \frac{1}{2016}\left(1 + \frac{1}{2014}\right).$$

Compare with $1 + \dfrac{2}{2015}$:

$$\frac{1}{2016}\left(1+\frac{1}{2014}\right) = \frac{2015}{2016\cdot 2014} \approx 0.00049628, \qquad \frac{2}{2015} \approx 0.00099256.$$

Since $2015 \cdot 2015 < 2 \cdot 2016 \cdot 2014$ (i.e. $2015^2 = 4{,}060{,}225 < 8{,}120{,}448 = 2\cdot 2016\cdot 2014$), we have

$$\frac{2015}{2016\cdot 2014} < \frac{2}{2015},$$

so $t < 1 + \dfrac{2}{2015} = \dfrac{2017}{2015}$, giving

$$\frac{2017}{t} > \frac{2017}{2017/2015} = 2015.$$

**Conclusion.** Combining both bounds,

$$2015 < \frac{2017!}{1!+2!+\cdots+2016!} < 2016,$$

so the integer part is

$$\boxed{2015}.$$

